-## 2026-08-23 — Closed the `integrate_overheat_response` `>=`-vs-`>` boundary gap named by the post-NDEBUG-fix audit below
-
-- **Why:** the "Deliberate mutation audit of `simulation_core_tests.cpp`'s post-NDEBUG-fix assertions" entry immediately below recorded a genuine, standing coverage gap: `integrate_overheat_response()`'s `temperature_k >= max_operating_temperature_k` comparison could be weakened to `>` without any test catching it, because the only existing crossing test intentionally overshoots the limit (`std::ceil`-rounded ticks) and can never land `temperature_k` bit-exactly on the threshold. That entry deliberately left the gap open rather than adding new `SimulationCore` configuration surface area speculatively, since a test-quality audit should not decide that on its own.
-- **Fix:** added `SimulationCore::set_max_operating_temperature_k(double)` (validated strictly positive, mirroring `set_energy_generation_w()`/`set_passive_cooling_w_per_k()`'s existing hardware/loadout-configuration-hook pattern; not a player-facing maneuver command, so it emits no domain event itself). This lets a test move the threshold to meet an unchanging `temperature_k` exactly, rather than trying to land closed-form thermal integration bit-exactly on a moving target: a probe with zero allocated power starts already at thermal equilibrium (`temperature_k == ambient_temperature_k` by construction), so `integrate_thermal_load()`'s delta-from-equilibrium term is an exact `0.0` and `temperature_k` cannot drift at all across any elapsed duration.
-- **New test:** `simulation_core_tests.cpp` reads the probe's own starting `temperature_k`, sets `max_operating_temperature_k` to that exact value, advances one tick with no power allocated, and asserts `temperature_k` is unchanged (`==`, not `nearly_equal`) while `is_overheated` is now true, `can_scan`/`can_thrust` are now false, and exactly one `OverheatStarted` fired. A weakened `>` would leave `is_overheated` false at exact equality, so this test fails under that mutation. A second new test covers `set_max_operating_temperature_k()`'s own validation (zero and negative rejected, existing threshold left untouched).
-- **Verification:** both new production branches were confirmed by targeted mutation against the exact CI command (`cmake -S src/simulation -B build/simulation -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release && cmake --build build/simulation --parallel 2 && ctest --test-dir build/simulation --output-on-failure`) — (1) weakening `>=` to `>` was caught cleanly by the new boundary test (`Assertion 'boundary_core.snapshot().is_overheated' failed`); (2) disabling the new setter's validation guard was caught cleanly by the new validation test (`Assertion 'zero_threw' failed`) — each reverted and diffed byte-identical before the next. The full suite, including both new tests, passed clean afterward (`100% tests passed, 0 tests failed out of 1`), as did a strict `g++ -std=c++20 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic` compile and `tools/quality_preflight.py --full`.
-- **Scope:** `src/simulation/` only — one new configuration hook, two new tests, no change to any existing production behavior or existing test's expected values. Does not touch `unreal/Source/`: no Unreal Editor/UBT toolchain was available in this environment, matching every other simulation-core-only slice recorded in `PROJECT_STATUS.md`.
-- **Prevention:** when a documented coverage gap is left open specifically because closing it would require adding new configuration/test surface area "speculatively," treat that named gap as the next well-scoped slice to actually close, rather than re-running broader audits that were already completed. The new setter is deliberately narrow (exactly the one threshold the gap named) rather than opening broader speculative configuration surface area.
-
-## 2026-08-23 — Deliberate mutation audit of `simulation_core_tests.cpp`'s post-NDEBUG-fix assertions
-
-- **Why this audit:** the entry directly below closed the pre-NDEBUG-fix-era gap (movement/tick, `ScanCommand`, `PowerAllocationCommand`) but explicitly declined to re-audit `EnergyConsumption` onward, trusting each later PR's own self-reported mutation testing rather than independently re-verifying it. This entry closes that remaining, self-admitted trust gap: every production code path introduced from `EnergyConsumption` through `SubsystemOperationalState` (lines ~202-950 as of this audit) — energy balance (drain, recharge, clamps, `EnergyDepleted`/`EnergyRestored` transitions), thermal load (closed-form heating/cooling, equilibrium), overheat lockout (threshold crossing and recovery), the combined-lockout stacking derivation, per-subsystem operational state (shedding, rejection, no-op, granular command ownership), the scan-pause-while-locked fix, and the canonical-probe factory.
-- **Method:** identical to the entry below — sixteen targeted mutations, each isolated to one production code path, applied one at a time to `core.hpp`, rebuilt and re-run via the exact CI command, then reverted and diffed byte-identical before the next mutation.
-- **Result:** 15 of 16 mutations were caught cleanly: `integrate_energy_balance`'s net-rate sign, its floor clamp, its ceiling clamp, its `EnergyDepleted` and `EnergyRestored` transition guards; `integrate_thermal_load`'s equilibrium-formula sign and its exponential decay-rate formula; `integrate_overheat_response`'s recovery branch; `refresh_capability_lockouts`'s `||` combinator and each subsystem's individual gating; `set_subsystem_operational`'s allocation-shedding and its same-state no-op guard; `allocate_power`'s operational gate; `integrate_scan`'s `can_scan` check (confirming the 2026-08-22 pause-while-locked fix immediately below stays regression-protected); and `make_canonical_ev0001`'s generation wiring.
-- **Finding, a real gap, not fixed here beyond a partial closure:** mutating `integrate_overheat_response`'s threshold comparison from `>=` to `>` was **not** caught. The existing crossing test deliberately rounds its tick count up (`std::ceil`) to guarantee landing at-or-past the limit, so it always overshoots strictly and never exercises the boundary at exact equality; forcing a genuine bit-exact `temperature_k == max_operating_temperature_k` through the public API is impractical given the closed-form exponential integration combined with discrete integer ticks (confirmed by direct search: no tick within a wide window around the analytically-derived crossing point lands bit-exact). Closing this fully would need a way to force an exact boundary hit — e.g. a fixed-point construction using the zero-cooling fallback path — which in turn needs configuration hooks for `max_operating_temperature_k` and `thermal_capacity_j_per_k` that do not exist and are out of scope for a test-quality audit to add speculatively. Recorded here as a standing, currently-impractical-to-close coverage gap rather than papered over: a future change that weakens the overheat comparison to strict-greater would not currently be caught by any test.
-- **Real gap that was fixed:** while constructing the case above, this audit found `integrate_thermal_load`'s `cooling_w_per_k <= 0.0` fallback branch (pure waste-heat accumulation with no passive-cooling pathway) had zero test coverage of any kind — `passive_cooling_w_per_k` had no configuration hook and every existing test relied on the struct's own nonzero default, so the branch was unreachable from this file entirely, not merely under-tested. Fixed by adding `SimulationCore::set_passive_cooling_w_per_k(double)` (validated non-negative, mirroring `set_energy_generation_w`'s own hardware/loadout-configuration-hook pattern) and a new `ThermalLoad` test exercising both the validation and the fallback branch's accumulate-without-bound behavior across two steps plus a zero-power hold. This new test was itself mutation-checked (disabling the fallback branch, and separately disabling its validation guard) before being accepted, confirming it is not tautological.
-- **Verification:** all sixteen mutations and their reverts are captured above; the final revert was diffed against the pre-audit file (`diff` reported no differences) and the full suite, including the two new assertions, was rebuilt and re-run clean (`100% tests passed, 0 tests failed out of 1`) before this entry was written. `tools/quality_preflight.py --full` also passed in full afterward.
-- **Prevention / scope:** this closes the self-admitted "not re-audited here" gap the entry below left open for `EnergyConsumption` onward. It does not claim the two `EnergyGeneration`/`CanonicalProbe`-era mutations already spot-checked here (`make_canonical_ev0001`'s wiring, `set_energy_generation_w`'s validation — both caught cleanly) constitute exhaustive per-assertion coverage of every line in those blocks, only that the major distinct production code paths were each exercised by at least one targeted mutation.
